{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>The Elohim of my father Avraham.</b> It would have been fitting that he mention first the special Name and say: 'YHWH, the Elohim of my father Avraham, and the Elohim of my father Yitzhak, who said to me.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: if he had said so, the special Name would have been an allusion to teshuvah, which is the attribute of judgment (din). But Yaakov was speaking in prayer and did not wish to enter within the inward side of Ḥesed — for everything that is within the inward side of Ḥesed is din and the line of uprightness — and he was afraid lest sin be caused. On this account he began with 'Elohei avi Avraham,' which is the attribute of Ḥesed, and concluded with the special Name, which is the attribute of prayer, and it is the attribute of mercy. Therefore he arranged the attributes in his prayer in this order: 'Elohei avi Avraham' — this is the attribute of Ḥesed; 'veElohei avi Yitzhak' — this is the attribute of Gevurah; 'YHWH' — the attribute of mercy. And it is in the language that we say in prayer: 'Elohei Avraham, Elohei Yitzhak, veElohei Yaakov.' And so it is written in the order above: 'ויאמר ה' אל יעקב שוב אל ארץ אבותיך ולמולדתך ואהיה עמך' (Genesis 31:3). And from this he mentioned here 'ואיטיבה עמך.'" "<b>And you already</b> know the saying of the masters of truth: the Patriarchs, they themselves are the Chariot, and there is no Chariot less than four, yet they call 'Patriarchs' only three — for the Holy One, blessed be He, called Himself 'Elohei Avraham, Elohei Yitzhak, veElohei Yaakov,' and we do not find the like concerning other prophets, that He should say 'the Elohei Moshe,' 'the Elohei Yonah' — even though we do find (Exodus 32:11) 'ויחל משה את פני ה' אלהיו,' and similarly in Yonah (Jonah 2:2) 'ויתפלל יונה אל ה' אלהיו' — nevertheless the Holy One, blessed be He, did not set apart His name upon them so as to be called 'the Elohei of so-and-so,' except upon Avraham, Yitzhak, and Yaakov. But we do find this regarding David, for it is written (II Kings 20:5) 'Elohei David avikha.'" "<b>And from this</b> the Rabbi, the father of the Kabbalah, Rabbi Yitzhak, son of the Rabbi, of blessed memory, was accustomed to say in his prayer 'Elohei David u-voneh Yerushalayim,' so that he would mention in his prayer the Chariot in its completeness with four Patriarchs. And the verse that says (ibid. 14) 'איה ה' אלהי אליהו' does not belong to this matter, for it is prayer. And the reason the Sages did not include David among the Patriarchs — their intention was in correspondence to the four living creatures of the Chariot, of which Scripture explicitly specified the place of only three: for it is written in the Chariot of Yeḥezkel (Ezekiel 1:10) 'ודמות פניהם פני אדם ופני אריה אל הימין לארבעתם ופני שור מהשמאל לארבעתם ופני נשר לארבעתם.' And the meaning of 'ודמות פניהם פני אדם' is that it is in the middle; and Scripture specified that on the right are the faces of a lion, on the left the faces of an ox — but the face of an eagle, which is the fourth, it did not mention its place at all, whether on the right or on the left, or in front or behind — for it is the attribute that is fitting to conceal. Therefore the masters of truth followed this path and mentioned only three Patriarchs."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:13.425684165Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}